1
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,000
Hey. What are you doing?

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,000
Hi.

3
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:10,420
Well, you know, I was thinking of moving the couch over here.

4
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:12,010
Why would you want to do that?

5
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,630
So that there would be a decent place for me to sit?

6
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:20,180
Rach, there is a decent place.

7
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:20,180
And your lap does not count.

8
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,810
Okay? Come on, help me move this.

9
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,810
No, no, no.

10
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,270
No?

11
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,270
No.

12
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:28,190
Rosita does not move.

13
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:33,740
I'm sorry. Rosita? As in...?

14
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,240
As in "Rosita does not move."

15
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:38,620
Joey, it's just a chair. What's the big deal?

16
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:42,540
The big deal is that it's the exact equal distance from the bathroom to the kitchen...

17
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:47,620
...and it's at the perfect angle so you don't get any glare coming off of Stevie.

18
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:50,090
Stevie the TV?

19
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:54,630
Is there a problem?

20
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:54,630
No.

21
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,090
Oh, what does he know?

22
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:02,350
Come on, Rosita. Us chicas gotta stick together.

23
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:07,770
Ow!

24
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:13,230
You bitch.

25
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:54,020
Hey, Ross?

26
00:01:54,280 --> 00:01:56,190
So I was checking out the real-estate section.

27
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,650
Yeah.

28
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,650
Look at this.

29
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,660
Oh, looks like Mom and Dad's house.

30
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:04,080
Oh, it even has a tree with a broken limb out front.

31
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:07,700
And the, uh, window in the attic...

32
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:10,790
Oh, my God.

33
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:14,250
What? What happened to the window in the attic?

34
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:16,960
I can't believe Mom and Dad are selling the house.

35
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:18,920
I can't believe they didn't even tell us.

36
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:22,390
I can't believe I still don't know what happened to the window in the attic.

37
00:02:23,890 --> 00:02:25,890
Okay, come on. I'll buy you a new one, all right?

38
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,350
We'll go down to the store right now and we'll get you a new chair.

39
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:35,110
She's not even cold yet.

40
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:39,990
But don't you think Rosita would've wanted you to move on?

41
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:44,780
I mean, you know, she did always put your comfort first.

42
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,370
That's true.

43
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,000
Wow.

44
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:56,590
I can't believe we have to say goodbye to the house we grew up in.

45
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:59,630
Man, some stranger's gonna be living in my room.

46
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:03,260
Well, after 15 years of Mom and Dad keeping it as a shrine to you...

47
00:03:03,430 --> 00:03:05,800
...it's time the velvet ropes came down.

48
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:08,220
They kept your room a while.

49
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:13,190
Oh, please. Dad turned my room into a gym 20 minutes after I moved out.

50
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:17,520
And I gotta say, a tanning bed and a stack of Victoria's Secret catalogs?

51
00:03:17,690 --> 00:03:18,980
Not a gym.

52
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:23,200
Come on. You know they love you.

53
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:23,200
As much as they love you?

54
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:27,530
I was their firstborn. They thought she was barren. It's not my fault.

55
00:03:30,790 --> 00:03:32,580
Ugh. I hate this year.

56
00:03:32,870 --> 00:03:34,500
What's wrong with this year?

57
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:37,420
It's already February and I've only given two massages...

58
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:40,510
...and they were both the worst tippers in the world.

59
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:42,130
That was me and Ross.

60
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:46,430
Oh, that's right.

61
00:03:47,010 --> 00:03:48,760
If you want to pick up some extra cash...

62
00:03:48,930 --> 00:03:52,480
...some friends of mine made good money in college doing telemarketing.

63
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:55,150
Yeah, it'd probably be better than the last telephone job I had.

64
00:03:55,310 --> 00:03:58,190
You know, I probably won't have to say "spank" as much.

65
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:03,570
What?

66
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:06,870
Oh, yeah, like you never called.

67
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:16,580
So, basically, this is very easy. You read from the script...

68
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:19,340
...and try to sell as much toner as you possibly can.

69
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:21,210
Okay, I can do that.

70
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:24,840
Oh, by the way, I love my office.

71
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:24,840
Ha, ha.

72
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:28,140
Why don't we do a trial run?

73
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:28,140
Oh, okay.

74
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:31,680
Um, all right.

75
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:35,020
Hi, this is Phoebe from Empire Office Supplies.

76
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:37,310
Can I speak to your supply manager, please?

77
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:39,060
I'm the supply manager.

78
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:41,480
Um, okay. I'd like to talk to you about your toner needs.

79
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:44,860
We don't need any toner.

80
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:44,860
Well, I'm sorry to bother you. Bye-bye.

81
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:47,660
Yeah, you're right. This is easy.

82
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:50,580
Okay. What was wrong with that call?

83
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:52,290
Oh, well, all right.

84
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:55,910
Um, no offense, but you were kind of rude.

85
00:04:57,460 --> 00:05:00,420
They're always gonna tell you they don't need toner, but that's okay.

86
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:01,960
Because whatever they say...

87
00:05:02,130 --> 00:05:04,380
...you can find the answer to it, here in the script.

88
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:07,720
Oh.

89
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:07,720
So I think you're ready to sell toner.

90
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:10,140
Do you have any last questions?

91
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:10,140
No.

92
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,890
Oh, wait, yes, I do. I do have one question:

93
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,310
What is toner?

94
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,100
Joey?

95
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:22,400
Joe?

96
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:28,740
Full bag?

97
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:32,700
Beer's still cold.

98
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,160
Something terrible must have happened here.

99
00:05:45,670 --> 00:05:47,420
Oh, no, no, no.

100
00:05:50,430 --> 00:05:53,060
Stevie, I was never here.

101
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,690
Dad?

102
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,690
I'm here.

103
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:01,810
Hey.

104
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:01,810
Hi.

105
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:06,320
God, it seems like just yesterday you guys used to come out to watch me work.

106
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:08,860
Dad, we can't believe you're selling the house.

107
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:11,240
It's time for a new family to start their memories here.

108
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:14,450
And hopefully their check will clear before they find the crack...

109
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:17,450
...in the foundation and the asbestos in the ceiling.

110
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:22,330
Let's just grab our stuff and get the hell out of here.

111
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:25,340
I'm sorry we can't store your childhood things anymore.

112
00:06:25,510 --> 00:06:28,720
Oh, it's okay. I can't wait to see everything again. All the memories.

113
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:30,800
I don't know what's in the boxes down here.

114
00:06:30,970 --> 00:06:34,720
But I do know that there are six or seven Easy-Bake Ovens in the attic.

115
00:06:35,810 --> 00:06:37,770
I used to love to play restaurant.

116
00:06:37,930 --> 00:06:41,060
Yeah, not as much as you loved to play "uncooked batter eater."

117
00:06:41,310 --> 00:06:46,650
Hey. It is unreasonable to expect a child to wait for a light bulb to cook brownies.

118
00:06:48,440 --> 00:06:51,110
So I think your boxes are over here.

119
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:52,530
Oh, great.

120
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:54,450
Hey...

121
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:56,660
...Dad, whose cigarettes are these?

122
00:06:58,830 --> 00:07:00,330
I don't know.

123
00:07:01,170 --> 00:07:03,710
They must be your mother's.

124
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:08,420
But please, please don't ask her. I'll throw these away.

125
00:07:12,470 --> 00:07:15,550
Cool. Dad, my report cards.

126
00:07:16,100 --> 00:07:17,390
Hey, check this out. Dad.

127
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:22,890
Math, A. Science, A. History, A. Gym...

128
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:31,570
Ooh, my rock polisher.

129
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:37,030
Uh-oh.

130
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:37,030
What?

131
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:39,740
You know how the garage floods every spring?

132
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:43,080
How are you ever gonna sell this place?

133
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:46,170
I think I accidentally used Monica's boxes...

134
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:48,800
...to keep the water away from the Porsche.

135
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:54,800
Oh, no, Dad. Dad?

136
00:07:54,970 --> 00:07:56,180
What the...?

137
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:58,180
Oh, God.

138
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:00,560
Everything's ruined.

139
00:08:00,890 --> 00:08:03,020
Dad, she's gonna be crushed.

140
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:06,980
You don't secretly smoke, do you?

141
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:10,400
No.

142
00:08:10,570 --> 00:08:12,610
So it's just your mother, then.

143
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:19,490
Hi. This is Phoebe from Empire Office Supplies.

144
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:22,700
Can I speak to your supply manager, please?

145
00:08:23,870 --> 00:08:25,710
Earl. Thanks.

146
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:30,550
Hi, Earl. This is Phoebe from Empire Office Supplies.

147
00:08:30,710 --> 00:08:33,420
I'd like to talk to you about your toner needs.

148
00:08:33,630 --> 00:08:36,090
I don't need any toner.

149
00:08:37,090 --> 00:08:41,810
I'm hearing what you're saying, but at our prices, everyone needs toner.

150
00:08:42,270 --> 00:08:43,890
Not me.

151
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,770
May I ask why?

152
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:51,570
You want to know why?

153
00:08:51,730 --> 00:08:53,570
I surely do.

154
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:55,400
Okay.

155
00:08:56,200 --> 00:08:58,950
I don't need any toner...

156
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:00,990
...because I'm gonna kill myself.

157
00:09:11,750 --> 00:09:13,340
Um...

158
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:16,720
Is that because you're out of toner?

159
00:09:20,930 --> 00:09:26,310
Okay, so no toner today. Thanks anyway. Bye-bye.

160
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:29,560
No, no, wait, wait. I can't just let you hang up.

161
00:09:29,730 --> 00:09:30,900
Just please talk to me.

162
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:32,900
Well...

163
00:09:33,860 --> 00:09:35,780
I only had one thing to do today.

164
00:09:38,990 --> 00:09:41,620
I guess I could push it back.

165
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:45,200
Yeah. Now, why do you want to kill yourself?

166
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:49,830
I've been working for 10 years now at this...

167
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,130
...meaningless, dead-end job...

168
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,210
...and nobody here even knows that I exist.

169
00:09:56,550 --> 00:09:58,130
Chandler?

170
00:10:02,470 --> 00:10:04,600
I'm sorry?

171
00:10:05,140 --> 00:10:07,680
Oh. Look...

172
00:10:07,850 --> 00:10:10,520
...I'm sure that people know you exist.

173
00:10:10,690 --> 00:10:14,150
Oh, yeah? I work in a cubicle surrounded by people.

174
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:19,110
I've been talking to you for five minutes now about killing myself...

175
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:22,570
...and no one's even looked up from their desk. Hang on.

176
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:24,580
Hey, everybody, uh...

177
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:26,870
...I'm gonna kill myself.

178
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,290
I got nothing.

179
00:10:35,550 --> 00:10:37,550
Good chair.

180
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,510
Now, if anybody asks...

181
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:42,550
...your name is Rosita.

182
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:08,660
Poor thing. Cut down in her prime.

183
00:11:10,290 --> 00:11:12,920
Joey, the new chair will be here in an hour.

184
00:11:13,080 --> 00:11:15,880
Maybe we should actually move Rosita out of here, you know?

185
00:11:16,050 --> 00:11:18,210
Start the healing process.

186
00:11:19,260 --> 00:11:20,920
Yeah, I guess you're right.

187
00:11:19,260 --> 00:11:20,920
Okay.

188
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:44,530
She's healed.

189
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:51,040
That's weird.

190
00:11:48,700 --> 00:11:51,040
No, it's not weird.

191
00:11:51,210 --> 00:11:53,040
It's a miracle.

192
00:11:54,170 --> 00:11:57,250
It's not a miracle, Joey. I'm sure there's an explanation.

193
00:11:57,420 --> 00:12:00,000
There is. If you want something enough and your heart is pure...

194
00:12:00,170 --> 00:12:01,510
...wondrous things can happen.

195
00:12:04,050 --> 00:12:07,100
Well, she'll understand, right? It's not like I did it on purpose.

196
00:12:07,260 --> 00:12:08,850
Dad, that won't matter to her.

197
00:12:09,010 --> 00:12:13,140
Look, all my stuff is safe and dry. And all her stuff is...

198
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:16,350
...growing new stuff.

199
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:18,310
See, this is exactly the kind of thing...

200
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:21,440
...that makes her think you guys love me more than you love her.

201
00:12:21,610 --> 00:12:23,610
Oh, my God. Does she really think that?

202
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:25,240
Well, can you blame her?

203
00:12:25,410 --> 00:12:28,740
I don't know. I suppose we may have favored you unconsciously.

204
00:12:28,910 --> 00:12:30,240
You were a medical marvel.

205
00:12:30,410 --> 00:12:33,290
The doctors said your mom couldn't...

206
00:12:30,410 --> 00:12:33,290
I don't want to hear about it.

207
00:12:33,460 --> 00:12:36,170
Really?

208
00:12:33,460 --> 00:12:36,170
Well, not right now.

209
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:39,170
Okay, look. Monica came here for some memories.

210
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:41,130
Damn it, we're gonna give her some.

211
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:42,920
Okay, grab...

212
00:12:43,090 --> 00:12:46,010
Grab some empty boxes, okay? We'll take stuff from mine...

213
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:49,930
...and whatever we can pass off as hers, we'll put them in there.

214
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,850
Right.

215
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:53,850
Like, this. She could have made this.

216
00:12:54,020 --> 00:12:56,310
This could have been hers.

217
00:12:54,020 --> 00:12:56,310
Sure.

218
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:02,320
You know, if you want to tell the medical marvel story while we work...

219
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:04,030
...that'd be fine.

220
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:11,330
I am so psyched I kept this chair for myself.

221
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:17,250
Yeah. Me too.

222
00:13:19,710 --> 00:13:23,210
Wow, you know, that this thing has speakers in the headrest?

223
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:26,340
No, really?

224
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:26,340
Yeah.

225
00:13:26,510 --> 00:13:29,010
You can hook it up to your TV and you get radio.

226
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:36,730
My chair heals itself.

227
00:13:39,230 --> 00:13:42,320
All right. Earl, let's just forget about the people at the office, okay?

228
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:46,190
There's gotta be someone else in your life that's worth sticking around for.

229
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:50,200
What about your family or friends? Or maybe a girlfriend?

230
00:13:50,370 --> 00:13:52,240
Heh. Yeah, right.

231
00:13:52,410 --> 00:13:54,540
Oh, sorry. Boyfriend?

232
00:13:55,750 --> 00:13:57,040
Okay, I should...

233
00:13:57,210 --> 00:13:59,830
I should probably be getting back to my thing now.

234
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,460
See you.

235
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:01,460
No.

236
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:04,460
I'm not finished yet. Don't you dare hang up on me!

237
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:07,380
The new girl's good.

238
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:11,850
So which boxes are mine?

239
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:15,060
These are yours right here.

240
00:14:12,010 --> 00:14:15,060
Okay.

241
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:18,980
Oh, a coloring book.

242
00:14:15,220 --> 00:14:18,980
Yeah, yeah. Oh, you loved that thing.

243
00:14:19,140 --> 00:14:22,110
You always had it with you. You never went anywhere without...

244
00:14:22,270 --> 00:14:23,900
Without that coloring book.

245
00:14:24,070 --> 00:14:25,360
Really?

246
00:14:25,530 --> 00:14:28,190
Wow. Looks like I had some trouble staying inside the lines.

247
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:30,450
Uh-uh.

248
00:14:31,490 --> 00:14:32,820
Wow, look at this.

249
00:14:32,990 --> 00:14:35,660
I can't believe I even fit into this shirt.

250
00:14:40,330 --> 00:14:41,880
Oh, this is yours.

251
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:44,630
I don't know how that got in there.

252
00:14:46,510 --> 00:14:48,590
Hey, this isn't my stuff.

253
00:14:48,760 --> 00:14:52,220
Ross, these are your boxes.

254
00:14:55,680 --> 00:14:57,390
Where are my boxes?

255
00:14:58,390 --> 00:15:01,980
The garage flooded, sweetie, and it ruined everything in your boxes.

256
00:15:02,150 --> 00:15:03,520
I'm sorry.

257
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:06,820
Just mine?

258
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:06,820
I'm afraid so.

259
00:15:07,230 --> 00:15:10,360
So why wasn't Ross' stuff ruined?

260
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:14,910
I used your boxes to divert water away from the Porsche.

261
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:17,910
So wait. Ross' stuff is fine, but I have no memories...

262
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:21,040
...because you wanted to keep the bottom two inches of your car from water?

263
00:15:21,210 --> 00:15:23,830
Well, there was also leaves and gook and stuff.

264
00:15:26,750 --> 00:15:28,090
I can't believe this.

265
00:15:34,930 --> 00:15:37,140
Screw it. I'm having one.

266
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:43,900
Hey, Chandler.

267
00:15:42,060 --> 00:15:43,900
Hey.

268
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:47,440
How would you like to sit in a chair that fully reclines...

269
00:15:47,610 --> 00:15:49,730
...has a rolling massage...

270
00:15:49,900 --> 00:15:52,200
...and speakers in the headrest?

271
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:54,610
Yeah, I'd love to, but I've tried that so many times...

272
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:57,530
...they won't even let me in the store anymore.

273
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,370
Well, what if I told you that you could do it in my apartment?

274
00:16:02,540 --> 00:16:03,870
Are you telling me...

275
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:07,540
...that you bought the chair that is making all other lounge systems obsolete?

276
00:16:07,710 --> 00:16:12,420
The chair that Sit Magazine called "Chair of the Year"?

277
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:15,390
I just purchased...

278
00:16:15,550 --> 00:16:19,680
...the La-Z-Boy E-Cliner 3000.

279
00:16:19,850 --> 00:16:22,180
Well, that's awesome. That's great. What made you do it?

280
00:16:22,350 --> 00:16:25,350
Well, it's a long story, but I broke Joey's chair...

281
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:28,270
Whoa, whoa, whoa. You broke Joey's chair?

282
00:16:28,440 --> 00:16:30,190
Yeah.

283
00:16:28,440 --> 00:16:30,190
I thought I broke Joey's chair.

284
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:33,490
That's why I replaced it with mine.

285
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:33,490
Oh.

286
00:16:33,650 --> 00:16:35,990
That's how it got fixed.

287
00:16:36,410 --> 00:16:39,530
Well, what did you think? That elves came in and fixed it?

288
00:16:39,740 --> 00:16:41,330
No.

289
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:43,120
Angels.

290
00:16:44,330 --> 00:16:47,330
I'm getting my chair back.

291
00:16:44,330 --> 00:16:47,330
Hey.

292
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:52,800
Well, looks like it wasn't healed after all.

293
00:16:53,340 --> 00:16:56,680
So I guess this chair's mine now.

294
00:16:59,850 --> 00:17:01,600
Joey, you broke my chair!

295
00:17:01,770 --> 00:17:02,810
Your chair?

296
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:06,020
Yeah, he thought he broke your chair, so he switched the chairs.

297
00:17:06,190 --> 00:17:09,860
So there was no miracle?

298
00:17:06,190 --> 00:17:09,860
No, Joe. No miracle.

299
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:12,190
Oh, no. This is devastating.

300
00:17:13,990 --> 00:17:15,030
My faith is shaken.

301
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:18,410
I'm so glad I have the new chair to get me through this difficult time in my life.

302
00:17:20,990 --> 00:17:23,450
Excuse me, could you tell me where can I find Earl?

303
00:17:23,620 --> 00:17:25,200
He's the supply manager here.

304
00:17:25,370 --> 00:17:26,620
Sorry, I don't know any Earl.

305
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:29,250
I'm right here!

306
00:17:32,550 --> 00:17:33,960
Earl?

307
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:38,720
I'm Phoebe.

308
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:38,720
Phoebe?

309
00:17:38,890 --> 00:17:41,300
The lady who sells toner?

310
00:17:41,470 --> 00:17:44,180
Um, look, you can't kill yourself.

311
00:17:44,890 --> 00:17:47,600
Look, um, I really appreciate you coming down...

312
00:17:47,770 --> 00:17:49,480
No, no, I can't let you do it.

313
00:17:49,770 --> 00:17:50,810
Why?

314
00:17:50,980 --> 00:17:53,940
Because it was fate that made me call you today.

315
00:17:54,150 --> 00:17:55,860
I thought it was toner.

316
00:17:56,570 --> 00:18:01,620
No. Think about it, okay? This isn't even my regular job, okay?

317
00:18:01,780 --> 00:18:04,540
And my first day on the job, you're my first call.

318
00:18:04,700 --> 00:18:07,790
And somebody else might have hung up on you, but I wouldn't do that...

319
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:10,330
...because I know about this stuff.

320
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:12,670
My mom killed herself.

321
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:12,670
Really?

322
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,460
Yes.

323
00:18:12,840 --> 00:18:14,460
How?

324
00:18:14,630 --> 00:18:16,260
I'm not gonna give you tips.

325
00:18:18,260 --> 00:18:20,180
Look, don't you see that this...?

326
00:18:20,340 --> 00:18:24,140
This all came together so that I could stop you from doing this.

327
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:28,520
Couldn't it just be a coincidence?

328
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:28,520
No, it's fate.

329
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:32,690
It doesn't really seem like enough to be fate.

330
00:18:32,860 --> 00:18:36,360
Oh, well. Um, okay, here's a weird thing.

331
00:18:36,530 --> 00:18:40,700
My mother was also a supply manager.

332
00:18:42,030 --> 00:18:43,870
I'm actually the office manager.

333
00:18:44,030 --> 00:18:46,240
Oh, my God. So was she!

334
00:18:46,410 --> 00:18:48,790
And get this, okay.

335
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:52,750
Your name is Earl, right? Her name was Pearl.

336
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:54,880
Pearl.

337
00:18:56,130 --> 00:18:57,840
Was there anything else?

338
00:18:58,010 --> 00:18:59,420
Sure.

339
00:19:00,510 --> 00:19:02,890
Um, where are you from?

340
00:19:03,050 --> 00:19:05,720
Philadelphia.

341
00:19:03,050 --> 00:19:05,720
Oh, my God, so was she!

342
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:09,430
Oh, I've got goose bumps.

343
00:19:09,770 --> 00:19:10,850
Really?

344
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:13,650
Well, I'm wearing layers, and it's warm.

345
00:19:11,020 --> 00:19:13,650
Yeah.

346
00:19:13,810 --> 00:19:17,190
But... Look. Okay. These jerks might not care about you...

347
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:21,200
...but the universe does, and that says a lot.

348
00:19:23,240 --> 00:19:24,990
Did you hear that?

349
00:19:25,410 --> 00:19:27,870
I don't need you guys to care about me...

350
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:30,330
...because the universe cares.

351
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:32,710
The whole universe!

352
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:40,550
I really wish they'd care just a little bit.

353
00:19:42,630 --> 00:19:44,970
Oh, this is terrible.

354
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:47,720
Everything is destroyed.

355
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:49,560
Look at this.

356
00:19:49,730 --> 00:19:53,640
It obviously meant enough for me to save it, and now I can't tell what it is.

357
00:19:54,900 --> 00:19:57,150
It's still soft.

358
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:01,940
What do you think this is?

359
00:20:03,410 --> 00:20:05,070
Mon, I think it was a mouse.

360
00:20:13,460 --> 00:20:16,290
How are you, honey?

361
00:20:13,460 --> 00:20:16,290
How do you think I am?

362
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:19,050
You've wrecked all my childhood memories...

363
00:20:19,210 --> 00:20:21,050
...you love Ross more than me.

364
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:24,470
And I just rubbed a dead mouse on my face.

365
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:30,470
Sweetheart, we love you just as much as Ross.

366
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:32,640
Now, I'm sorry about everything that happened...

367
00:20:32,810 --> 00:20:36,440
...and I'll probably never be able to make it up to you...

368
00:20:36,610 --> 00:20:38,480
...but here's a start.

369
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:44,450
What's this?

370
00:20:41,320 --> 00:20:44,450
It's the key to my Porsche.

371
00:20:44,610 --> 00:20:46,780
Well, the key to your Porsche.

372
00:20:47,990 --> 00:20:50,910
What?

373
00:20:47,990 --> 00:20:50,910
What?

374
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:53,790
I've been thinking of getting rid of it...

375
00:20:53,960 --> 00:20:57,080
...and I was driving it the other day and I saw my reflection in a window.

376
00:20:57,250 --> 00:21:00,420
Your mother's right, I do look like an ass.

377
00:21:01,590 --> 00:21:04,220
You're giving it to me? You're kidding me, right?

378
00:21:04,380 --> 00:21:07,430
Well, wait a minute.

379
00:21:07,590 --> 00:21:11,310
I mean, a couple of stupid boxes get wet, and she gets a Porsche?

380
00:21:13,390 --> 00:21:15,480
Why don't we take it for a spin?

381
00:21:13,390 --> 00:21:15,480
All right.

382
00:21:15,640 --> 00:21:19,310
What about me? I'm a medical marvel!

383
00:21:27,610 --> 00:21:29,280
Oh, yeah.

384
00:21:27,610 --> 00:21:29,280
Oh.

385
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,120
Hey, guys.

386
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,120
Hey.

387
00:21:35,290 --> 00:21:37,160
Know what happened to Chandler's Barcalounger?

388
00:21:37,330 --> 00:21:39,380
Oh, yeah. Joey broke it. Had to get rid of it.

389
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:44,090
Are you kidding? I get a Porsche and the Barcalounger's gone?

390
00:21:44,260 --> 00:21:47,130
Heh. This is the best day ever!

